  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 228 AM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nThroughout the day today, the axis of the upper level ridge will \nshift east over Colorado, allowing warmer air to advect into the \nregion. 700-mb temperatures should reach up to 15-17 dg C which \nroughly translates to temperatures in the high 80s to low 90s \nacross the plains. With the upper level ridge shifting eastward, \nwe will also start drying out over most the forecast area. This, \ncombined with warming aloft, will result in less thunderstorm \nactivity this afternoon, although CAMs still favor a few storms \ndeveloping over the Front Range and spilling onto the adjacent \nplains. Given less moisture availability, main concerns for any \nstorm over the mountains and I-25 Corridor will be strong outflow\nwinds. \n\nThere is also a conditional threat for severe thunderstorms over the \nfar northeastern plains later in the afternoon/evening hours. Models \nhint at a weak 700-mb shortwave north/northeast of Colorado, \nwhich should provide forcing for thunderstorm activity over \nNebraska. While CAMs keep most of the storms out of Colorado \n(with the 06Z runs less aggressive with thunderstorm activity \ncompared to the 00Z runs), can't rule out an isolated storm \ndeveloping over the far northeastern plains (10-20% chance), \nespecially since that area will be able to hang onto the low-level\nmoisture and thus have better instability throughout the day. \nGiven MLCAPE values up to 1000-1500 J/kg and 0-6 Km bulk shear of\n40 kts, storms would be capable of producing hail and strong \nwinds. \n\nWarmer temperatures arrive by Friday and Saturday as the upper \nlevel ridge continues shifting eastward, with highs reaching the \nlow-to- mid 90s both days. A strong upper level low over the \nPacific Northwest will cause flow aloft to increase and turn \nwest/southwest over Colorado. This will create breezy conditions \nover the high terrain, but also re-introduce mid-level moisture \ninto the region. On Friday, we could see isolated high-based \nshowers and thunderstorms. But, Saturday afternoon looks better \nfor thunderstorm activity. Convection should be high-based, with \nonly a few storms producing beneficial rainfall. \n\nTowards the end of the weekend/beginning of next week, the upper \nlevel low will trek across the U.S./Canada border, with embedded \nshortwaves moving over Colorado. \"Cooler\" temperatures (if you \nconsider 80s to be cooler) will arrive, along with better low- \nlevel moisture. Ensemble guidance continues to favor storms \nSunday and Monday, but looks to have backed off for Tuesday due to\nan exiting shortwave. This could still change depending on the \nevolution of the multiple disturbances, so will keep an eye on it.",
